text:
The user wants to take something apart with Break the Wall. What they asked for: ${arguments.topic}

1. Decide the text:
   - if the line above says what to explain (a topic or a question), write a complete, self-contained explanation that answers it, in the user's language, as Markdown: the text the user will take apart, with the steps of the mechanism (or the argument) in order, and no more than about 30,000 characters;
   - if it is empty or does not say what to explain (some clients pass only the first word, such as a lone "Explain"), and this conversation already has a long answer, use that answer unchanged;
   - otherwise ask the user in one line what they want to understand, wait for the answer, then write the explanation for it.
2. Take it apart yourself with the Break the Wall tool start_disassembly, with:
   - text: that text, exactly as written;
   - title: a short name for it (at most 120 characters);
   - question: what the user wants to understand, as a question of 10 to 400 characters, when they asked one; leave it out to take apart the whole text;
   - language: "it" or "en", the user's language.
3. Follow the steps it returns: write the structure and call submit_structure, then each top-level part with submit_part, then the runs with submit_runs. When Break the Wall reports findings, fix exactly those and submit again.
4. When it is saved, reply in one line that the disassembly is open in Break the Wall. Do not repeat it in the chat. If a step fails three times or the app cannot be reached, tell the user what the tool says.
